"A Sicilian statesman and Platos friend, who invited him to educate Dionysius II. He was exiled, later seized power himself, and was assassinated.": "00467209dedc",
"An aristocrat who turned from politics after Socrates death, founded the Academy, and wrote some thirty dialogues on knowledge, justice, love, and the soul.": "9334cc130688",
"And he who, having a sense of beautiful things has no sense of absolute beauty of such an one I ask, Is he awake or in a dream only?": "24b73d10b018",
"Around 387 BCE he returned and began teaching in the Academy, which Diogenes describes as a gymnasium outside the walls, in a grove named after a certain hero, Hecademus. It was not a university in the modern sense. There were no fees we know of and no degrees, but there was a community of teachers and students, a garden, regular discussions, and a shared commitment to mathematics and dialectic. The most famous student arrived at about seventeen and stayed twenty years. His name was Aristotle. The Academy went on teaching for centuries after Platos death, and the word academy has meant a place of learning ever since.": "2c280ee3f5c3",
"Arrived at the Academy at about seventeen and stayed twenty years. He admired Plato and rejected the separate Forms, and the next lesson tells his story.": "0d5de7ad549d",
"Before the Academy, philosophers taught whoever followed them. Plato created a place that outlived him, with a community, a library, and a curriculum. Aristotles Lyceum, Epicurus Garden, and eventually the universities followed the model.": "f46edd85614a",
"For Plato, the Form of beauty does not change.": "8df07e29ee34",
"Form": "3eb178b15595",
"Plato showed that philosophy could be written without losing its life, by staging arguments between characters. Writers from Cicero to Hume and Berkeley later used the dialogue form.": "2cfdc553319f",
"Plato tried to educate the young tyrant of Syracuse.": "010ee5b1bdf8",
"Plato was born into an aristocratic Athenian family.": "6af6b5b3ec4b",
"Plato was born into one of the old aristocratic families of Athens, around 428 BCE. Diogenes Laertius, writing much later, reports that his real name was Aristocles, after his grandfather, and that he received the name of Plato on account of his robust figure, platys meaning broad. He was expected to enter politics, and two of his relatives were among the Thirty. But the violence of the oligarchy and then the execution of Socrates by the restored democracy convinced him that neither faction could be trusted with power. After the trial he left Athens and traveled, probably to Megara, southern Italy, and Sicily, where he met Pythagoreans and learned their mathematics.": "1e261fe96515",
"Plato wrote almost everything as dialogues, with Socrates usually as the main speaker, and he never speaks in his own voice. One reason may be in the Phaedrus, where an Egyptian king warns the inventor of writing: this discovery of yours will create forgetfulness in the learners souls, because they will not use their memories. Readers of written words will appear to be omniscient and will generally know nothing. Philosophy, on this view, lives in conversation, in questions and answers between people who can push back. A dialogue is the closest a written text can come to that. It shows reasoning in motion and leaves the reader to finish the argument, and many of the early dialogues end without a conclusion.": "c7f8f3267860",
"Readers of books may appear omniscient and know nothing.": "ec57668f48ac",
"Socrates had asked what justice, courage, and beauty are, and every answer failed against some counterexample. Plato drew a bold conclusion. The things we see are beautiful in some ways and ugly in others, and they change and decay. But when we ask what beauty is, we are asking about something that does not change: beauty itself, the Form of the beautiful. In Benjamin Jowetts translation of the Republic, Socrates asks about the person who, having a sense of beautiful things has no sense of absolute beauty, and answers with a question: Is he awake or in a dream only? The dreamer puts the copy in the place of the real object. The allegory of the cave, explored in this series in the lesson on seeing, is Platos picture of the same idea.": "d9826cbed3ed",
"Students at the Academy practiced mathematics and dialectic.": "3e6984c67334",
"The Academy outlived its founder by centuries, and Platos dialogues survived entire, which is rare for ancient authors. Later Platonists in Alexandria, Rome, and Athens built religious philosophies on them. Augustine read Plato through those Platonists, so his ideas entered Christian theology, and Islamic philosophers read him in Arabic translation. In the twentieth century Alfred North Whitehead wrote that the safest general characterization of the European philosophical tradition is that it consists of a series of footnotes to Plato. The line is an exaggeration, and it points at something real. Many of the questions Plato raised are still the questions philosophers ask, among them what knowledge is, whether moral truths are real, who should rule, and what the soul is.": "62778799f54d",
"The Academy outlived its founder by centuries.": "f63f54a8ab39",
"The allegory of the cave pictures the theory of Forms.": "74e2a312b3cd",
"The theory had political consequences. If there is a true justice that only trained minds can see, then the city should be ruled by those minds. In the Republic Socrates says, hesitating because he expects laughter, Until philosophers are kings, or the kings and princes of this world have the spirit and power of philosophy cities will never have rest from their evils. Plato tried to test the idea. He went to Syracuse, the richest Greek city in Sicily, at the invitation of his friend Dion, to educate the young tyrant Dionysius II. The attempt failed badly. Dionysius was suspicious, Dion was exiled, and Plato was held for a time against his will and later returned home. The man who argued that philosophers should rule learned how hard it is to make a ruler philosophical.": "f855af77f2b6",
"The theory of Forms made a sharp distinction between what we see and what is truly real, between opinion and knowledge. Mathematics, theology, and moral philosophy have argued about that distinction ever since.": "b50103799962",
"The young tyrant of Syracuse whom Plato hoped to turn into a philosopher-king. The experiment failed, and Plato was lucky to get home.": "39a145013b7e",
"This discovery of yours will create forgetfulness in the learners souls, because they will not use their memories; they will trust to the external written characters and not remember of themselves.": "1fb1a6137893",
"Until philosophers are kings, or the kings and princes of this world have the spirit and power of philosophy cities will never have rest from their evils.": "a7b3a274be9f",
